---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
// Privacy page (milestone 8, round-1 user-test fix 1: the round-1
// panel found no privacy page at all). Every statement below is true
// of the site as built and shipped today: there is no account system
// and no upload endpoint; the tool programs run in the visitor's
// browser; and since milestone 19 the visits are counted with
// Google Analytics, named and explained in its own section below
// (the milestone-19 copy replaced this page's earlier "no analytics
// script runs today" position in the same step that installed the
// tag, so the page never claimed one thing while the site did
// another). Since milestone 23 the ad network is Google AdSense,
// named in the ads section below in the same step that put its
// loader on the pages, as the earlier wording on this page
// promised: the network is named before its code runs. If any of
// that changes, this page changes first, and it says so below.
---

<Base title="Privacy · Benchzy" description="What Benchzy keeps and what it does not: no accounts, no file uploads, visits counted with Google Analytics, ads served by Google AdSense. Your files are processed in your browser and never sent to us.">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4">
    <section class="mt-8">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-accent-deep">Privacy</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl font-black mt-2">What this site keeps, in plain words</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
        Benchzy is built so that your files never reach us. Every tool on this site runs as a
        program inside your browser, on your device. There is nothing to upload to, and no
        account to sign up for, so the short version is: we never see your files, your name, or
        your email address, because the site never asks for them and has no way to receive them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">What the server keeps</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 space-y-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft max-w-3xl">
        <p>
          The host is named here, not hidden behind a phrase: this site is served by
          Cloudflare Pages. The hosting service keeps routine request logs: which page or
          program file was asked for, when, and the technical details a browser sends with
          every request (such as its type and your network address). Those logs exist so the
          site can be served and protected from abuse. They contain no files, because no file
          is ever sent.
        </p>
        <p>
          How long anything is kept: we keep no logs of our own, none at all. The host's
          connection logs exist on the host's own schedule, which the host sets and can
          change; this page will not invent a number of days for someone else's system.
        </p>
        <p>
          There are no accounts on Benchzy, so there is no account data. No tool on this site
          has an upload: pick a file in any tool and it is read by the page in your browser,
          never transmitted. Two outside scripts do run when a page opens, and both are named
          on this page, in the open: one counts visits (its own section is next), and one is
          the ad network's loader (the ads section below names the network).
        </p>
        <p>
          Passwords, for the two tools that ask for one (protect a PDF, unlock a PDF):
          passwords are used in the page only and are gone when the page closes. They are
          never typed into anything that sends them anywhere, never stored, and never logged
          by us.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">Counting visits, in plain words</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 space-y-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft max-w-3xl">
        <p>
          Visits to these pages are counted with Google Analytics, a visit counter run by
          Google. It is how we learn which tools get used and which pages nobody finds, so we
          know what to build and what to fix. This page names it rather than letting it ride
          along quietly: the counter was switched on only when this section was written to
          say so.
        </p>
        <p>
          What the counter sees: that a page was opened, which page it was, roughly where in
          the world the connection comes from, and what kind of device and browser asked.
          That is the ordinary information a page opening carries, and Google keeps the
          counts on its own systems, under its own rules.
        </p>
        <p>
          What it never sees: your files. The counter fires when a page opens. It is not part
          of any tool, it is never told a file's name, size or contents, and running a tool
          sends it nothing at all. The
          <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/check-it-yourself/">check-it-yourself page</a>
          lists the counter's own line beside every other request the page makes, so you can
          watch exactly that.
        </p>
        <p>
          Counting is not something a tool needs, so you can block it and lose nothing: a
          content blocker or your browser's tracking protection stops the counter like any
          other, and Google publishes its own opt-out add-on (the Google Analytics opt-out
          add-on) for the same job. Every tool on this site works exactly the same with the
          counter blocked.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">What your browser stores on your device</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 space-y-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft max-w-3xl">
        <p>
          The batch tools and the workflow builder keep a small workbench list in your
          browser's local storage: the names and sizes of files you added, the presets you
          saved, and the workflow steps you built. That list lives on your device only; it is
          not sent anywhere, and it holds names and sizes, never the files themselves.
        </p>
        <p>
          Your browser also keeps a cached copy of the tool programs themselves (for example
          the 44.3 MB background-removal program), so repeat visits start without downloading
          them again. That cache holds program files only.
        </p>
        <p>
          To clear all of it: use your browser's "clear site data" or "clear browsing data"
          option for benchzy.com. That removes the workbench list, your presets and workflows,
          and the cached programs in one go. Saved result files you already downloaded are
          ordinary files on your device and stay yours to keep or delete.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">Ads, stated plainly</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 space-y-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft max-w-3xl">
        <p>
          The site is paid for by ads on the pages, and the ads are served by Google
          AdSense. It is named here, as this page promised it would be, before its code went
          on the site: the AdSense loader program is on every page now, and it loads when a
          page opens, like the visit counter. What it can see is the visit itself, the same
          ordinary information a page opening carries: that a page was opened, which page it
          was, roughly where in the world the connection comes from, and what kind of device
          and browser asked. Google serves the ads and keeps what it collects on its own
          systems, under its own rules.
        </p>
        <p>
          One thing will not change: an ad can never see your file. That is not a promise about
          physics; it is how this site is built. Your file is processed by the tool program
          inside your browser and is never sent to any server, ours or an advertiser's, so
          there is no copy of it for an ad to see.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 mb-4">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">What we do not protect you from</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft max-w-3xl list-disc pl-6">
        <li>Your own device. If your computer or phone is compromised, or someone else uses it, anything on it, including files you processed here, is exposed to them.</li>
        <li>Your browser extensions. Extensions you installed can read the pages you visit, including this one.</li>
        <li>Your network seeing the visit itself. Your internet provider, employer, or Wi-Fi operator can see that you visited benchzy.com. They cannot see your files, because your files never travel.</li>
        <li>What you do with a result afterwards. Once you download a finished file and share it, where it goes is up to you.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft max-w-3xl">
        You can check the technical claims on this page yourself: the
        <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/check-it-yourself/">check-it-yourself page</a>
        watches a live run and lists every request the page makes, and
        <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/what-free-means/">what free means here</a>
        states the deal in full.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
